be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 AM MST Sunday through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.28dceea107844d87e801d5ba537294d1bcbe9f7e.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 31 at 5:44PM MDT until November 2 at 9:10PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"...The Flood Warning continues for the following rivers in\nColorado...\n\n...This is a PARTICULARY DANGEROUS SITUATION...\n\nFor the San Juan River at Pagosa Springs affecting Archuleta County.\n\n.Significant and historic flooding is possible along the San Juan\nRiver at Pagosa Springs. Widespread rainfall is expected to begin\nearly Sunday morning and continue through Monday morning. Excessive\nrunoff will also increase flooding risks. The region should prepare\nfor a dangerous and impactful flood event.\n\nFor the San Juan River...including Pagosa Springs...major flooding is\nforecast. This is a particularly dangerous situation.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding caused by excessive rainfall is forecast\nalong the San Juan River. This is a Particularly Dangerous\nSituation.\n\n* WHERE...San Juan River at Pagosa Springs.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday afternoon to Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 17.0 feet, Portions of San Juan Street are beginning\nto flood.  Waters have reached the foundation of the Colorado\nRanger Building.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 5:00 PM MDT Saturday the stage was 6.6 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 8.5 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage\ntomorrow afternoon to a crest of 17.0 feet early Monday morning.\nIt will then fall below flood stage Monday afternoon.\n- Flood stage is 10.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 17.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T21:10:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.41a09bda1714f4d5c7f26254e1cfcd17fe8ea772.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.41a09bda1714f4d5c7f26254e1cfcd17fe8ea772.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-01T11:54:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 011154 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 454 AM MST Sun Nov 1 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - The Flood Watch continues for southeast Utah and southwest Colorado as an atmospheric river brings rain, rainshowers, and snowfall to the area. - Several rivers across southwest Colorado are forecast to reach minor flooding stage. - The San Juan River at Pa","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-01T06:36:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-012200- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1235 AM MDT Sun Nov 1 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"},"coagmet":{"station_id":"pgs01","station_name":"Pagosa Springs","distance_km":2.6,"soil_temp_5cm_f":47.28,"soil_temp_15cm_f":48.69,"soil_moisture_5cm":null,"soil_moisture_15cm":null,"precip_in":null,"eto_in":null,"vapor_pressure":null,"solar_radiation":18.07,"wind_speed_mph":1.5,"air_temp_f":43.68,"relative_humidity":0.9,"url":"https://coagmet.colostate.edu